From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 14:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kv-cache: a slot's rank decides whose cells hold the VRAM line

0040 moves a batch's cells below the tiered cache's VRAM line, but every
sequence is equal: a sequence that ran in one of the last n_seq_max
batches never moves, anything else may. With one model serving two
conversations and a child context (operator, 2026-09-28: 3 slots, the
first conversation never spills, the child swaps into VRAM while its
conversation's main pauses), that is wrong three ways: a second
conversation that arrives while the first is between two requests takes
the first one's VRAM; a child cannot take its paused main's cells for
n_seq_max batches (it spills meanwhile), nor the main its finished
child's; and two running conversations both claim the lowest cells.

Now each sequence has a RANK (higher keeps VRAM, default 0) and an ACTIVE
flag, told by the server (llama_memory_seq_set_rank):

- the request field `kv_rank` is the slot's rank from the moment its task
  starts, kept after it ends; `kv_ranks` (an array by slot id, < 0 =
  unchanged) sets the OTHER slots' ranks at the same moment, so a client
  that ranks its slots can demote a slot nobody is using. A slot is active
  from its task's launch to its release. /props reports `kv_vram_cells`.
- plan_moves groups the batch's sequences by rank, highest first; the
  first group whose span would pass the VRAM line and that can gain makes
  the plan (the next batch plans the next group). Against group G of rank
  r, a sequence keeps its cells (P) if its rank is higher -- running OR
  idle: a primary conversation between two requests keeps its VRAM -- or
  equal and active; everything else may move out: a lower rank even while
  it runs (a second conversation spills first), an idle one of G's rank
  (the main while its child runs, the child after it ends).
- eviction order: the cells exchanged with G's cells (they land where
  G's were, deepest in the host tier) are the lowest rank first, an idle
  owner before an active one; the cells left over move to the lowest
  empty cells just above G's new span, the highest rank first.

A sequence never told keeps 0040's rule (active = in one of the last
n_seq_max batches). LLAMA_KV_RANK=0 ranks every sequence alike and ignores
the active flags: 0040 exactly. Moves still happen only when a span would
pass the line (0040's determinism argument is unchanged). What this cannot
do: two sequences decoded in ONE batch share every step, so a spilled
lower-rank sequence's host staging still lengthens the step the higher
rank waits for; placement keeps the higher rank's own cells in VRAM, and
it runs at full speed alone. docs/ENGINES.md "KV rank".
